  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "What is the limit of the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(5n-3)(5n+3)}{n(2-25n)}$ for $n\\ge 1?$\n\n${lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "First, we multiply out the numerator and denominator of the sequence and get\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{(5n-3)(5n+3)}{n(2-25n)}=\\frac{25{n}^{2}-9}{2n-25{n}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo to find the limit, we will need to compute\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{25{n}^{2}-9}{2n-25{n}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe dominant term in the denominator is $-25{n}^{2},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{25{n}^{2}-9}{2n-25{n}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{25{n}^{2}}{{n}^{2}}-\\frac{9}{{n}^{2}})}{(\\frac{2n}{{n}^{2}}-\\frac{25{n}^{2}}{{n}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(25-\\frac{9}{{n}^{2}})}{(\\frac{2}{n}-25)} \\\\  & =\\frac{25-0}{0-25} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the sequence converges and its limit is $-1.$",
